.SH NAME
ktm-guide-reflection \- Reflection (chapter 24 of the ktm language guide)
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Reflection lets a program look at its own types while it runs: a struct's fields and where they are, an enum's members and their names, a value's type. The \fBreflect\fR module provides it. This chapter covers describing a type with \fBreflect.typeOf\fR, what a \fBreflect.Type\fR holds, handling a value of any type with \fBreflect.of\fR, reading and writing fields by name, enum names, attributes, and how the library builds on it.
.SH Describing a type
.PP
\fBreflect.typeOf(T)\fR gives a \fBreflect.Type *\fR for a type, or for the type of an expression:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import reflect;

enum Role { guest, admin = 10 };

struct User {
    i64 id;
    u8[] name;
    Role role;
    User *boss;
};

i32 main() {
    reflect.Type *t = reflect.typeOf(User);
    printf("%s, %d bytes, %d fields\en", t.name, t.size, t.fields.len);
    for (auto f : t.fields) {
        printf("  %\-5s at %2d: %s\en", f.name, f.offset, f.type.name);
    }
    reflect.Type *boss = t.fields[3].type;
    printf("%s points to %s\en", boss.name, boss.elem.name);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
User, 40 bytes, 4 fields
  id    at  0: i64
  name  at  8: u8[]
  role  at 24: Role
  boss  at 32: User *
User * points to User
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBf.offset\fR is the field's offset in bytes, which is how a C \fBoffsetof\fR is written in ktm.
.PP
The type of an expression works the same way:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
import reflect;

i32 main() {
    i64 grid[4];
    f32 ratio = 0.5;
    printf("%s %s %s\en", reflect.typeOf(grid).name, reflect.typeOf(ratio).name, reflect.typeOf(&ratio).name);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
i64[4] f32 f32 *
.EE
.RE
